  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "Owners in Mexico commonly look at a mix of **homegrown lifestyle brands** and **global soft-brand / design-led flags** when they want a property with distinctive character. The names that appear most often in the results are **Grupo Habita, Kimpton, Mondrian, Moxy, Mama Shelter, Hyde, The Hoxton, Hotel Indigo, Vignette Collection, Curio Collection by Hilton, and Design Hotels**.[2][4][6][18]\n\n- **Grupo Habita** is the clearest Mexican reference point: it is described as the country’s first lifestyle design hotel brand and a pioneering lifestyle hotel brand in Mexico.[2][3][10]\n- **Kimpton** is explicitly described as design-forward and is growing in Mexico, including Mexico City and Baja California Sur.[18]\n- **Mondrian** is presented as a brand rooted in art, design, and culture, with properties in Mexico City and Cancun.[4][7]\n- **Moxy** is being positioned in Mexico as youthful and design-forward, with Moxy Tulum emphasizing immersive, design-forward experiences.[12]\n- **Mama Shelter** is highlighted for eclectic energy and expressive design, and is opening in Mexico City.[6]\n- **Hyde** is described as music-forward, with vibrant positioning for Mexico City.[6]\n- **The Hoxton** is associated with curated programming and local art and retail experiences in Mexico City.[6]\n- **Hotel Indigo** is often considered for neighborhood- and culture-driven positioning, which fits character-led hotels.[18]\n- **Vignette Collection** is framed as a curation of distinct, hand-picked hotels for one-of-a-kind stays.[18]\n- **Curio Collection by Hilton** is used for distinctive, design-forward independent hotels, including the upcoming Amàre Cancun in the results.[14]\n- **Design Hotels** is frequently used as a benchmark for boutique, design-centric properties in Mexico.[5][11][19]\n\nIf you want, I can turn this into a **shortlist by use case**—for example, *most Mexican-authentic*, *most lifestyle-forward*, or *best fit for urban vs. resort projects*.",
